   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Haber plus the participle",
     "texto": [
      "The present perfect is formed with **he, has, ha, hemos, habéis, han** + **participle**. **Habéis** is used with vosotros, mainly in Spain; with ustedes use **han**.\n\n- **He vivido** aquí dos años y todavía vivo aquí. (The period continues now.)\n- ¿**Has estado** en México?\n\nRegular participles: **hablado, comido, vivido**. Very common irregulars: **visto, hecho, escrito, abierto, dicho, puesto**.\n\n**Ya** means something happened before now. **Todavía no** and **aún no** mean it has not happened yet.\n\nIn much of Spain it is used for events in a time that includes today: **hoy he visto** a friend. In many parts of America the indefinido is preferred in these cases. It changes by region."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "How the perfect is built",
     "texto": [
      "A recipe with two ingredients:\n\n- **Haber** in the present: **he, has, ha, hemos, habéis, han**.\n- **Participle**: **-ar → -ado** (hablado) and **-er / -ir → -ido** (comido, vivido).\n- Irregulars: **ver → visto**, **hacer → hecho**, **escribir → escrito**, **abrir → abierto**, **decir → dicho**, **poner → puesto**.\n\nThe participle **does not change** with haber: **he visto**, **hemos visto**. Nothing goes between **haber** and the participle: **ya he visto**, never «he ya visto». Pronouns go before: **lo he visto**.\n\n**Ya** goes before the verb: **ya he comido**. **Todavía no** and **aún no** too: **todavía no he comido**."
     ],
